    "blurb": "Features a pristine Desolation Row and a powerful Throwing Stones/NFA stretch in the second set. Jerry’s guitar recovers after early tone issues while Phil endures a broken string but keeps the groove. Overall an all‑around solid performance with good set flow.",
    "review": "On September 6, 1988 at the Capital Centre, the Dead delivered a reliably tight two‑set outing that rewards patient listening. The first set opens with a warm “Jack Straw” and rolls through classics before hitting a standout moment on “Desolation Row,” which many fans describe as flawless – a rare highlight for this era. A brief hiccup in Jerry’s tone early in the night gives way to a confident ride through the “Not Fade Away”‑powered Throwing Stones/NFA wave, where Brent’s keyboards and the dual drummers lock into an infectious groove. The second set’s climax with “The Other One” and a soaring “Throwing Stones” showcases the band’s improvisational chemistry, while the encore “Brokedown Palace” closes on a gentle, reflective note.\n\nWhile the Miller soundboard capture (gd88-09-06.sbd.miller.27750.sbeok.flacf) offers clear SBD fidelity with only slight crowd bleed, an audience tape (gd88-09-06.aud.ingle.9984.sbeok.shnf) adds a sense of live energy despite its tinny quality and occasional vocal muffs. Together they paint a picture of a solid performance that may lack the fireworks of a legendary night but still provides plenty of moments worth revisiting for any Deadhead.",
